RED: one declared MIR transport-shape failure, one semantic control PASS,
zero Rust compilation errors. The small C input is loop-value-transport.c.
The shared out-of-SSA pass now removes only trivial phi transport: all incoming
definitions agree, apart from unchanged self-forwarding. Distinct definitions,
swaps and counters retain their copies, even when their constant bits agree.
Entry bindings, local-SSA validation, accesses, guards and label identities stay.
EvoMIR 0.38 states this rule separately from unchanged physical allocation.

Inline-asm/asm.goto and computed-goto functions remain on the existing path.
The indirect-cycle regression caught why: a joint self-edge copy can overwrite
the source needed by another selected target. Its separate receiving slot and
saved branch register must remain. This negative is not weakened or deleted.

Tailored checks: four MIR tests include 144 exact pre-allocation executions,
counter/swap controls, different bindings to one target, and all five data
register classes under actual spilling. The independent allocation checker
passes. Existing cycle, indirect dispatch, critical-edge and deterministic
allocation tests pass. The C case checks nested loops, volatile counts, zero-trip
null-pointer controls and alternating values through both C-HIR/EvoIR at O0/O2,
MIR, native emission and allocation verification. Four interpreter and two MIR
executions return zero; all memory-operation and trap counts are retained.
GCC 14.2.0-19, Clang 19.1.7 and EvoCC O0/O2 native controls: six exit-zero PASS.

